---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CircuitDivider from '../components/CircuitDivider.astro';
import SurfaceCodeLattice from '../components/SurfaceCodeLattice.astro';
import ThresholdChart from '../components/ThresholdChart.astro';
import { profile } from '../data/profile';
import { url } from '../data/url';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Decoding the Surface Code"
  description="Interactive note on surface code decoding: syndrome extraction, minimum-weight decoding, and logical error rates for d = 3 and d = 5 rotated surface codes."
>
  <section class="container page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive note</p>
    <h1>Decoding the surface code</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The surface code encodes one logical qubit in a lattice of physical qubits and detects errors through repeated
      parity checks. This note shows syndrome extraction and minimum-weight decoding on d = 3 and d = 5 rotated surface
      codes, computed in the browser.
    </p>
    <p class="back"><a href={url('/research')}>← Research</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="container play">
    <div class="card lab reveal">
      <SurfaceCodeLattice />
    </div>
    <aside class="how reveal">
      <h2>Using the demo</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Apply errors.</strong> Choose X (bit flip), Z (phase flip) or Y (both), then select a data qubit.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Read the syndrome.</strong> Checks adjacent to an error report odd parity. As on hardware, the decoder
          sees only the checks, not the data qubits.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Decode.</strong> The decoder returns the lowest-weight error consistent with the syndrome; the proposed
          correction is shown with dashed rings.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Logical failure.</strong> A d = 3 code corrects any single error. Two X errors in the same column (or two
          Z errors in the same row) produce a syndrome that a single error also explains, so the correction completes a
          logical operator. 18 of the 36 two-qubit X errors fail in this way.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="note">
        Implementation: X and Z errors are decoded independently with an exact minimum-weight lookup table, built by
        breadth-first search over syndromes. This is tractable only for small codes; larger codes require matching
        decoders (e.g. PyMatching) or learned decoders.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <CircuitDivider />

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Threshold behaviour</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      Below the <strong>threshold</strong> error rate, increasing the code distance suppresses logical errors; above it,
      larger codes perform worse.
    </p>
    <div class="card reveal chart-card">
      <ThresholdChart />
    </div>
    <p class="caveat">
      Noise model: code capacity with independent bit flips and perfect syndrome measurement. This is the most favourable
      setting, so the crossing point is optimistic; with measurement errors and circuit-level noise, thresholds fall to
      roughly 1%.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section container why">
    <div class="reveal">
      <h2>Relation to my research</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        My research interest is <strong>machine-learning decoders that are accurate and fast enough for real-time error
        correction</strong>. This note covers the simplest version of that problem. Natural extensions are circuit-level
        noise with <a href="https://github.com/quantumlib/Stim" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stim</a>, matching
        baselines with <a href="https://github.com/oscarhiggott/PyMatching" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PyMatching</a>,
        and comparison against neural decoders trained on simulated syndromes.
      </p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={url('/research')}>Research & publications →</a>
        <a class="btn" href={`${profile.links.github}/my-website/blob/main/src/scripts/surfaceCode.ts`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source code ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .play {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.25fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 32px;
    align-items: start;
    padding-bottom: 48px;
  }
  .lab { padding: 24px 16px; }
  .back { margin: 0; font-size: 0.92rem; }
  .how h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; }
  .how ol { padding-left: 1.2em; color: var(--text-dim); display: grid; gap: 12px; margin: 0 0 20px; }
  .how strong, .lead strong { color: var(--text); }
  .note, .caveat { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text-dim); border-left: 2px solid var(--border); padding-left: 14px; }
  .caveat { margin-top: 20px; max-width: 75ch; }
  .chart-card { margin-top: 24px; }
  .why .lead { max-width: 70ch; }
  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 12px; margin-top: 8px; }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .play { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .lab, .chart-card { padding: 16px 10px; }
  }
</style>
